    "abstract": "To curb industrial air pollution, China introduced a major ultra-low emission upgrading program for the steel industry in 2019. While these upgrades are intended to improve environmental quality, less is known about their associated local economic benefits. Using housing transaction data matched with plant-level information, we find that upgrade completion is capitalized into nearby property values, increasing housing prices by about 6%. The absence of a detectable response to the policy announcement alone suggests that completed upgrades provide more concrete and verifiable information, reducing implementation uncertainty and information frictions. Further analysis suggests that the price effect reflects both improvements in local environmental quality and subsequent updates in residents' beliefs. These findings highlight the broader economic value of environmental upgrading in the steel industry and the importance of credible policy implementation.",
